WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 189: Jimmy Carter: The Hostage Crisis, the Killer Rabbit, and the Nobel Peace Prize (1979–Present)

1
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.760
[Rebecca] Today we conclude our Jimmy Carter saga.

4
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:20.160
[Rebecca] While his final year in office was engulfed by international crisis and bizarre media sensationalism, Carter's post-presidency redefined what it means to serve humanity.

5
00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:29.280
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Swamp Rabbit, the Iranian desert tragedy, and the Nobel Peace Prize.

6
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:52.080
[George] While enjoying a quiet, solitary fishing trip in a flat-bottomed aluminum John boat near Plains, President Carter spotted a large marsh rabbit swimming frantically through the murky water directly toward his boat.

7
00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:58.640
[George] Hissing with bared teeth and flaring nostrils, the terrified creature attempted to climb aboard the gunnel.

8
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:08.080
[George] Recognizing that the animal was panicked and might bite, Carter used his wooden paddle to splash water at the rabbit until it turned and swam safely back to shore.

9
00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:17.760
[George] Several months later, White House press Secretary Rex Graham casually mentioned the odd incident over drinks with associated press reporter Brooks Jackson.

10
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:24.720
[George] The Washington Post ran a sensational front-page headline on August 30: President attacked by Rabbit.

11
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:32.160
[George] Editorial cartoonists had a field day, drawing Carter fleeing from giant, fanged, carnivorous bunny monsters.

12
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:47.600
[George] When the White House released an authentic photograph taken by a Navy photographer showing Carter splashing water at the swimming rabbit, the killer rabbit was permanently etched into American political folklore, an amusing distraction in an era of gathering storm clouds.

13
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:59.280
[George] The hilarious rabbit folklore became an affectionate part of American political legend, showing how the intense media spotlight can magnify the most innocent personal encounters into worldwide news.

14
00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:08.440
[George] The media's humorous fascination with the swamp rabbit showed how folklore and quirky anecdotes can humanize leaders during times of great stress.

15
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:20.040
[George] In the summer of 1979, the United States was gripped by double-digit inflation, skyrocketing interest rates, and crippling gas lines sparked by the Iranian Revolution.

16
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:32.040
[George] Retreating to Camp David for ten solitary days, Carter met with dozens of civic leaders, labor officials, sociologists, and ordinary citizens to diagnose the national funk.

17
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:44.520
[George] On July 15, 1979, Carter addressed 65 million Americans on live television in what became known as the Malay's speech, though Carter never once uttered the word malaise.

18
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.200
[George] In an astonishingly candid, philosophical sermon.

19
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:53.000
[George] Carter diagnosed what he termed a crisis of confidence in the American soul.

20
00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:57.400
[George] The symptoms of this crisis of the American spirit are all around us.

21
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:05.480
[George] For the first time in the history of our country, a majority of our people believe that the next five years will be worse than the past five years.

22
00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:09.720
[George] Too many of us now tend to worship self indulgence and consumption.

23
00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:13.560
[George] The speech initially received enthusiastic public acclaim.

24
00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:26.280
[George] But when Carter abruptly asked for the resignations of his entire cabinet 48 hours later to reorganize his administration, the public panicked, viewing the reshuffle as a sign of chaos and paralysis.

25
00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:38.200
[George] That unvarnished diagnosis of the national spirit urged Americans to look beyond material consumerism and rediscover the foundational communal virtues of citizenship and shared sacrifice.

26
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:47.520
[George] Carter's speech reflected his courage to speak uncomfortable truths to the American public, challenging the nation to live up to its highest moral ideals.

27
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.720
[George] On November 4, 1979, international catastrophes struck.

28
00:03:52.720 --> 00:04:05.760
[George] In Tehran, hundreds of radical Islamist student militants stormed the United States embassy, furious that Carter had permitted the deposed, cancer-stricken Shah of Iran into the United States for medical treatment.

29
00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:18.880
[George] Fifty-two American diplomats and military guards were blindfolded, tied up, and paraded before television cameras, beginning a 444-day hostage ordeal that held the superpower hostage.

30
00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:27.440
[George] Nightly television broadcasts, led by Ted Capell's new ABC program, Nightline, counted the days with grim graphics.

31
00:04:27.440 --> 00:04:30.720
[George] America held hostage, day 100.

32
00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.320
[George] Day 200.

33
00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:40.640
[George] Carter adopted the Rose Garden strategy, refusing to travel or campaign for re-election while American citizens remained captive.

34
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:58.160
[George] He left the National Christmas tree dark except for the top star, held daily prayer vigils in the Oval Office, and resisted fierce political demands from Hawks to bomb Iranian oil fields, knowing that military strikes would result in the immediate execution of every American hostage.

35
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:09.680
[George] Carter's refusal to bomb Iranian targets protected the lives of the 52 hostages, prioritizing the survival of individual American citizens over domestic political expediency.

36
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:19.040
[George] His patient restraint during the 444 days kept the hostages alive, defying political pressure for rash military escalation.

37
00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:31.400
[George] By April 1980, after five agonizing months of diplomatic stalemate, Carter reluctantly approved Operation Eagle Claw, a daring, highly complex military rescue mission.

38
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:48.520
[George] Elite Delta Force commandos led by Colonel Charlie Beckwith were to fly by eight Navy RH-53 DC Stalliopters from the carrier USS Nimitz to a remote desert airstrip codenamed Desert One in central Iran, rendezvous with C-130 tankers.

39
00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:53.400
[George] Drive concealed in trucks into Tehran, and storm the embassy compound.

40
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.640
[George] But from the outset, the mission was cursed by horrific misfortune.

41
00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:06.280
[George] A blinding desert dust storm known as a Haboob disabled three helicopters, reducing the operational fleet below the minimum six required.

42
00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.360
[George] Beckwith recommended aborting the mission, and Carter concurred.

43
00:06:10.360 --> 00:06:22.280
[George] But as the aircraft prepared to withdraw in the swirling darkness, a helicopter collided with an EC-130 tanker aircraft, igniting a colossal fireball of aviation fuel and ammunition.

44
00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:26.280
[George] Eight brave American servicemen were killed in the desert sands.

45
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:35.080
[George] Carter went on live television the next morning with tears in his eyes, taking sole, unreserved personal responsibility for the tragic failure.

46
00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:47.000
[George] The tragic disaster at Desert One broke Carter's heart, yet his immediate, unreserved acceptance of full personal responsibility exemplified the highest standard of presidential accountability.

47
00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:55.960
[George] Carter's transparent honesty in addressing the tragic Desert One failure showed the profound moral accountability that defined his entire public life.

48
00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:09.960
[George] Crushed by the hostage crisis, 44% inflation, and a divided party, Carter was overwhelmingly defeated in the November 1980 presidential election by charismatic Republican nominee Ronald Reagan.

49
00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:19.920
[George] Yet in his final 72 days in office, Carter worked with heroic, non-stop dedication to secure the release of the hostages before his term expired.

50
00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:31.120
[George] Working through Algerian diplomats, Carter negotiated the Algiers Accords, unfreezing nearly $8 billion in Iranian assets in exchange for the hostage's safe return.

51
00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:40.240
[George] Carter went without sleep for 48 continuous hours in the Oval Office, fine-tuning the complex international bank transfers down to the final minute.

52
00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:58.640
[George] In a petty act of vengeance, the Iranian regime held the transport plane on the runway in Tehran until precisely 12.33 on January 20, 1981, 20 minutes after Ronald Reagan took the oath of office, before allowing the aircraft to take off into international airspace.

53
00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:12.960
[George] The following morning, President Reagan graciously dispatched former President Carter as his personal envoy to Wiesbaden, Germany, where a weeping Jimmy Carter hugged each of the 52 freed Americans, finally bringing them home alive.

54
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:25.040
[George] His heroic, around-the-clock negotiations in his final days in office ensured that every single American hostage was delivered safely home to their families without the loss of a single hostage's life.

55
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:33.440
[George] The release of the hostages on inauguration day brought profound relief to the nation and closure to an agonizing chapter in American history.

56
00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:45.120
[George] Returning home to Plains at age 56, bankrupt and facing $1 million in debt on his peanut warehouse, Carter refused lucrative corporate board seats and paid speeches.

57
00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:58.720
[George] Partnering with Rosalind, he founded the non-profit Carter Center in Atlanta, dedicated to advancing human rights, resolving armed conflicts, and eradicating neglected tropical diseases across the developing world.

58
00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:11.720
[George] In 1984, the Carters donned work boots, tool belts and safety goggles to volunteer with Habitat for Humanity, a fledgling Christian charity building affordable homes for impoverished families.

59
00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:26.200
[George] For 35 continuous years, through the annual Jimmy and Rosalind Carter Work Project, the former president spent a week every year pounding nails, hauling lumber, and hanging drywall beside thousands of volunteers.

60
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:32.120
[George] Personally building over 4,300 homes in 14 countries from India to Haiti.

61
00:09:32.120 --> 00:09:44.760
[George] Photographed with a black eye and 14 stitches after a fall at age 95, Carter refused to cancel his shift, building houses in Nashville the next morning with a bandaged face and a hammer in his hand.

62
00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:57.480
[George] The Carters' tireless work with Habitat for Humanity transformed millions of lives, demonstrating that genuine service to humanity is performed with deeds, sweat, and humility rather than titles.

63
00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:06.040
[George] The decades spent volunteering with Habitat for Humanity showed that true leadership is demonstrated through service to the poor and vulnerable.

64
00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:22.520
[George] Carter's greatest scientific and humanitarian triumph was his near-total eradication of guinea worm disease, a horrific, agonizing parasite that had afflicted three and a half million impoverished people across 21 nations in Africa and Asia in 1986.

65
00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:36.760
[George] Distributing millions of simple water filter straws and teaching villagers hygiene, Carter's campaign reduced global cases by an astonishing 99.9%, down to fewer than 15 cases worldwide today.

66
00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:43.720
[George] Poised to become only the second human disease in history, completely wiped from the face of the earth after smallpox.

67
00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:56.720
[George] In 2002, Jimmy Carter was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize for his decades of untiring effort to find peaceful solutions to international conflicts, advance democracy, and promote economic development.

68
00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:10.960
[George] Surpassing John Adams and Herbert Hoover to become the longest-lived president in American history, Carter entered hospice care in Plains in 2023 at the age of 98, surrounded by the red Georgia soil where his journey had begun.

69
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:22.480
[George] Jimmy Carter proved that an individual's true moral greatness is not defined by the fleeting tenure of political office, but by a lifetime of selfless, unfailing love for the human family.

70
00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:34.080
[George] The near-total eradication of guinea worm disease and the Nobel Peace Prize cemented Jimmy Carter's legacy as an immortal humanitarian whose life was an enduring beacon of selfless love.

71
00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:42.880
[George] His life of 98 years proved that an individual's enduring legacy is measured by the sum of human suffering eased and lives redeemed.

72
00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:51.200
[Rebecca] Jimmy Carter's extraordinary century of life proved that character, humility, and service to others outshine all the trappings of power.

73
00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:57.280
[Rebecca] To succeed him in 1980, the American nation turned to the great communicator, Ronald Reagan.

74
00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:11.840
[Rebecca] Next week, we begin our exploration of Ronald Reagan, the boy from Dixon, Illinois, the Hollywood actor, the 1981 assassination attempt, and his famous hospital humor, and the economic revolution that reshaped the 1980s.

75
00:12:11.840 --> 00:12:16.080
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

